<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bashir Shihab II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bashir Shihab II was emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> from 1789 to 1840, the most powerful ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shihab-dynasty">Shihab dynasty</a>. Born a Muslim, he became a Maronite Christian and broke the power of rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> lords. He built the palace of Beiteddine and sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a> in the 1830s. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> out in 1840, he was exiled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bashir-shihab-ii.jpg" alt="Bashir Shihab II" width="287" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daoud Corm d. 1930 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BashirChehab.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bashir was born on 2 January 1767 in Ghazir, a village in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keserwan">Keserwan</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>, the son of Qasim ibn Umar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shihab-dynasty">Shihab dynasty</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> nobility had chosen the Shihab family to hold the tax farm of Mount Lebanon when the Ma'an dynasty died out in 1697. In 1787, sent to Hasbaya to list the assets of Bashir ibn Najm, whom Emir Yusuf Shihab had killed, he married the dead man's wealthy widow Shams, gaining considerable wealth; they had three sons, Qasim, Khalil and Amin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Emir Yusuf lost the backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezzar-pasha">Ahmad Pasha al-Jazzar</a>, the Ottoman governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, and was routed at Jubb Jannin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> sheikhs, with the key support of the Jumblatt clan, chose Bashir as their hakim, and he received the tax farms at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in September 1789. He then removed his rivals: the five Abi Nakad sheikhs were ambushed and killed in his palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-al-qamar">Deir al-Qamar</a>, and on 15 May 1807 his former supporter Baz was strangled while Yusuf's sons were captured and blinded.</p><p>After 1821, when his son Khalil conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a> for Abdullah Pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, Bashir took the tax districts of the Jumblatts and redistributed them to his allies, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Hauran</a>. He centralised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> by setting a legal code based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> and handing civil and criminal cases to three judges he appointed himself, two of them Maronite clergymen.</p><p>When the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a> under Ibrahim Pasha invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1831, Bashir took the Egyptian side and suppressed revolts against conscription and disarmament, from Safad in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkar">Akkar</a> and the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latakia">Latakia</a> in 1834–1835. In August 1840 he rejected three offers from the British consul Richard Wood to change sides.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Bashir was the strongest of the Shihabi emirs, but his rule nearly destroyed the feudal power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> and Maronite lords on which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> rested and helped bring sectarianism into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>'s politics, above all when he used Maronite fighters against Druze risings and Druze fighters against Maronite risings. The historian William Harris holds that his half-century bequeathed the beginnings of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. Legend has it that he cut off the hands of the architect of his palace at Beiteddine so that it would remain unique.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bashir Shihab II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bashir II</b> (1789-1840), who became Christian, built the palace of Beiteddine and sided with Muhammad Ali of Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/mount-lebanon-emirate">Mount Lebanon Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ghazir, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.02,35.67&amp;year=1820" aria-label="Ghazir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bashir Shihab II"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5401 4647h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5401 4647h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.02,35.67&amp;year=1820">What was here in 1820? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
